{"kind":"artifact","text":"The trust assumption hugh ruled is written where a reader of the posture will meet it. Exact clean pushed head 802a7c31345129f7dfbeda447ec8ce57ced3e843 on request/env-trust-assumption; checkout clean, origin matching, base is current main 3a5d952c and the branch is one commit ahead of it, both verified at filing. Under planner's docs-only child 2996e429 and my promise dbe53373. Documentation only.\n\nWHERE IT WENT AND WHY THERE. The Host posture section, immediately after the paragraph that already says the supported posture is one trusted operator account and that direct local gs key access and malicious same-account processes are outside the resident's protection. The environment assumption is a sibling of that sentence, not a new topic, and a reader who has just been told which same-account threats are out of scope is exactly the reader who needs to know this one is too. I looked for an existing threat-model page first; there is none, and the conditions offered this section as the alternative.\n\nWHAT IT SAYS. That GIT_DIR, GIT_WORK_TREE and GIT_COMMON_DIR, which decide which repository Git resolves; GIT_CONFIG, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL, GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM and the rest of the GIT_CONFIG_* family, which decide what configuration it reads; and HOME and XDG_CONFIG_HOME, which locate several of those files, are all outside the threat model. Gitseq trusts the environment of the operator who runs it and defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal.\n\nIT GIVES THE REASON RATHER THAN ONLY THE RULE. Git configuration is not inert — core.fsmonitor, core.pager and diff.external name programs Git runs, and an include can reach a file outside the repository — so an attacker who can set these variables can already run code as the operator. That is the account the resident's keys live in, so a defence here would not protect anything such an attacker could not reach another way. A rule without its reason is one the next person re-litigates; this lane re-litigated it three times.\n\nIT ALSO SAYS WHAT THE CODE THAT BOUNDS THE ENVIRONMENT IS BUYING, because otherwise the page and the code read as disagreeing and a reader would have to guess which is stale. internal/app states the environment for the Git commands behind /v0/worktrees, and internal/gitstore's hermeticGitEnvironment pins the system and global configuration scopes. Both are determinism: a read that ignores the invoking shell answers for the repository it was pointed at, whoever started the process. Neither is a claim that the operator is an adversary.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO. I did not touch internal/gitstore, internal/app or any other code — this row is documentation only and the ruling it records explicitly declines the strip. I did not weaken or annotate hermeticGitEnvironment's existing behaviour; I described it.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached, internal/docset uncached at 79.203s since a documentation page changed. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet and git diff --check all exit 0.","body":{"commit":"802a7c31345129f7dfbeda447ec8ce57ced3e843","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}